 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bosch HBS534BS0B oven displaying E011 and won't let me do anything. Beeping. Help!"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "I understand your Bosch oven is beeping and locked up with an E011 code. On Bosch ovens, this error typically points to a control issue—either a button on the panel is stuck down (preventing the computer from registering new inputs) or there's a fault with the temperature sensor or its connection. Since you can't operate it right now, let's try a simple check first. Please press each button on the control panel individually to see if any feel physically stuck or unresponsive. Let me know exactly what happens when you do this."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Pressed them all, nothing responds, just keeps beeping. Nothing feels stuck. Worth saying it started about an hour ago right after I wiped the panel with a wet cloth and kitchen spray."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "The timing suggests that the kitchen spray and wet cloth caused moisture to seep into the control panel, creating a short circuit. This is why the buttons aren't responding—the computer sees 'stuck' signals from the damp electronics. Please switch off the oven at the wall (using the cooker switch or pulling the plug) for 10 minutes to let any internal moisture evaporate and reset the electronics. Then turn it back on and see if the beeping stops and the display returns to normal."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, dried the panel with a towel and switched off at the cooker switch for 5 minutes. Came back up normally, no code. Heated to 180C fine too."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news. It suggests the E011 error was caused by moisture from the kitchen spray seeping into the control panel and confusing the electronics. Since it has reset and heated correctly, you have successfully fixed it yourself. To prevent this happening again in future, try to avoid spraying cleaning products directly onto or near the control panel; instead, apply them to a cloth first and wipe gently without leaving it damp. If the error returns despite keeping the panel dry, then there may be an internal fault with the sensor or wiring, but for now you are good to go."
  }
 ],
